# 5. Heading / H1 handling

Every heading-related site:

```ts
// twyne-editor.tsx:80-82 — paragraph-format style map
  "paragraph.heading-1": "h1",
  "paragraph.heading-2": "h2",
  "paragraph.heading-3": "h3",

// :693 — schema limit
              heading: { levels: [1, 2, 3] },

// :727
              types: ["heading", "paragraph"],

// :918-920 — active-state readback
            h1: editor.isActive("heading", { level: 1 }),
            h2: editor.isActive("heading", { level: 2 }),
            h3: editor.isActive("heading", { level: 3 }),

// :947-948
          const paragraphAttrs = editor.isActive("heading")
            ? editor.getAttributes("heading")

// :960 — note: a heading is ALWAYS treated as keep-with-next
          paragraphAttrs.keepWithNext === true || editor.isActive("heading");

// :2750-2761 — command dispatch
        // heading a heading would not match anyone's expectation.
        case "h1":
          chain.toggleHeading({ level: 1 }).run();
        case "h2": ... level: 2 ...
        case "h3": ... level: 3 ...
```

### Things that look like real H1 hazards

**(a) Schema allows only h1–h3, but the outline walks h1–h6.** `document-outline.ts:4-5` sets `MIN_HEADING_LEVEL = 1; MAX_HEADING_LEVEL = 6`, and `section-reorder.ts:106` queries `closest("h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6")`. Harmless today, but the two disagree.

**(b) Every drop near the top of the document resolves to the H1.** `section-reorder.ts:126` — `let target = outline.flat[0];` with no guard for "coordinates are above the first heading."

**(c) The H1 has a `::after` pseudo-element and is centered** (`global.css:748-768`):

```css
.twyne-editor .ProseMirror h1 {
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: 2.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  margin-top: 1.2em;
  margin-bottom: 0.45em;
  line-height: 1.1;
  color: var(--color-ink);
  text-align: center;
}

.twyne-editor .ProseMirror h1::after {
  content: "✦";
  display: block;
  text-align: center;
  color: var(--color-vermilion);
  font-size: 1rem;
  margin-top: 0.3em;
  letter-spacing: 0.5em;
}
```

The `::after` is a `display: block` generated box **inside** the contenteditable `<h1>`. That is a classic source of caret/selection anomalies: clicking below the H1 text but on the ✦ row resolves to a position ProseMirror does not expect, and `view.posAtCoords` inside `headingAtEvent` (`section-reorder.ts:121`) will resolve those pixels to the H1. It also collides with the `SectionReorder` drag handle, which is `position: absolute` at the heading's *static position* — for a `text-align: center` H1 that static position is the centered line origin, so the ⋮⋮ handle lands **1.5rem left of the centered text**, i.e. floating in the middle of the page rather than in the left margin, unlike for h2/h3.

**(d) The drop cap interacts with H1 ordering** (`global.css:738-746`):

```css
.twyne-editor .ProseMirror > p:first-of-type::first-letter {
  font-family: inherit;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: min(4.2em, 5rem);
  float: left;
  line-height: 0.85;
  margin: 0.08em 0.12em -0.05em 0;
  color: var(--color-vermilion);
}
```

`:first-of-type` matches the first `<p>` child regardless of whether an H1 precedes it — so a document starting with an H1 still gets a floated drop cap on the paragraph under it, and the float interacts with the H1's `::after` block box directly above.

**(e) `keepWithNext` is forced true for all headings** (`twyne-editor.tsx:960`), which feeds the pagination engine — an H1 near a page boundary will always push to the next sheet.

